class.description:
The session creates one [NetwPlayer] when it accepts a join and keeps that object until the player leaves, so it can be compared with [code]==[/code] and used as a [Dictionary] key. A connected peer that has not joined has none.
[codeblock]
var player := session.player_of(peer_id)
greet(player.username)
for entity: NetwEntity in player.entities:
    follow(entity.root)
[/codeblock]
A player is not a peer. After the player leaves, [member is_active] is [code]false[/code], even if a new player connects with the same [member peer_id].
Every join is a new player, including a join with an [member account] that joined before. A game that wants to recognise a returning player keys its own data by [member account] on the server, which is empty for a guest.
[codeblock]
var scores := {}

func on_player_joined(player: NetwPlayer) -> void:
    if player.account != "" and not scores.has(player.account):
        scores[player.account] = 0
[/codeblock]
[member account] reaches only the server and the player's own peer. Every other peer reads it empty and still reads [member account_name].

signal.description:
Emitted when [member scene] changes. Either may be [code]null[/code]. Only [member NetwSession.local_player] emits it.
[codeblock]
func _ready() -> void:
    Netw.session(self).local_joined.connect(on_local_joined)

func on_local_joined(me: NetwPlayer) -> void:
    me.scene_changed.connect(on_scene_changed)

func on_scene_changed(_from: NetwScene, to: NetwScene) -> void:
    lobby.visible = to == null
[/codeblock]
